  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 205 PM CDT Mon Sep 28 2026\n\nUpper ridge axis is observed from the southern plains stretching \ninto the Midwest this afternoon. Meanwhile the closed upper low is \nrotating through CA while upstream moisture is increasing into the \nCO Rockies and southwest CONUS. Hurricane Polo remains off the Baja \nCalifornia coastline, anticipated to weaken as it amplifies the \nmoisture transport into New Mexico and eventually the Plains on \nTuesday. For this evening, stratus deck gradually fills back in \novernight similar to this morning's setup with lows in the lower \n60s. Fog is not expected to be a concern as sfc south winds increase \nby sunrise from 10 to 20 mph sustained. Initial rain chances begin \nTuesday late afternoon and evening towards north central Kansas as \nsouthwest flow aloft deepens ahead of a slow-moving cold front. A \nstrong low level jet and ample moisture transport persists into \nWednesday and Thursday where PWAT values climb to near 2 inches, \nrepresenting greater than 99th percentile on the NAEFS and EC \nensembles. The combination of the low level frontogenesis with deep \nwarm cloud layer depths and strong moisture transport are increasing \nconfidence in flooding concerns. In addition, MUCAPE increases to \nnear 1500 J/KG, introducing chances for thunderstorms in north \ncentral KS and therefore higher rainfall rates. Forecast rainfall \namounts are generally 1 to 3 inches area wide with locally higher \namounts of 4 to 5 inches north of I-70. This is where a Flood Watch \nhas been hoisted from Tuesday evening through Thursday. \n\nWhile most convection holds below severe limits, there could be a \nfew severe storms Wednesday afternoon and evening as the sfc trough \nenters the state amid a dryline positioned north to south in western \nKansas. Location of the sfc low and degree of forcing within the \nwarm sector of eastern Kansas varies among guidance, lending to low \nconfidence in location and threats for severe weather Wednesday.\n\nThe main upper trough ejects northeast into the Upper Midwest by \nThursday night, pushing the cold front through the state. Dry \nconditions return Friday into the weekend as surface high develops \nthroughout the central conus. Highs Friday through Sunday average in \nthe upper 60s, as clearing skies bring overnight lows down into the \nlower 40s.",
